#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1adb26c2-291f-5ecb-be27-a83497de5b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238279} {:answer/id #uuid "a4933581-9ab8-5174-ab43-a4a7af154ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} {:answer/id #uuid "add0b6a3-0052-52a8-a4b1-57278ac77df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239563} {:answer/id #uuid "b0815d5d-2eab-5eb6-b0b6-cea74e67c474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239588} {:answer/id #uuid "bc186f6a-3c28-5f17-b90f-9614a603b361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4933581-9ab8-5174-ab43-a4a7af154ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f03b38-7ac7-5b2a-bebf-d97ba46b022c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b025c1d-a393-5ea5-8c4e-6e0786f6f25d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104617" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a528a36-e97e-5732-8fae-1697a81d8ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238153} {:answer/id #uuid "44bbd131-4ecd-540b-ad14-259cefa4dcf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238651} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab4b784-0b5f-5e1b-9fde-ec17a7e2873d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238701} {:answer/id #uuid "89f290a7-499e-5b23-8f65-ac96513f25dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239255} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb83a5e-cf58-56d7-bc9a-928b68a83802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecb83a5e-cf58-56d7-bc9a-928b68a83802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbad1630-befd-5b0d-9389-d98b46343814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1eb7495c-4bb1-5f06-bc1d-e458481de613" :question/math-academy-id "q-104623" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}\\cdot \\pi )-({0}^{2}\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =(2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f31b92-c386-50b2-b977-d94225bcfbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238253} {:answer/id #uuid "465fbdf4-7176-5e2a-afb9-7055988a9594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238665} {:answer/id #uuid "54e54a33-5402-591a-ac33-570a9653db6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238789} {:answer/id #uuid "796fc17c-d543-5b01-83ae-f85bfdb65ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239102} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd66caa-a9be-51dd-a8aa-57947da3bed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54e54a33-5402-591a-ac33-570a9653db6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238789} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed60cbe-d21d-5310-989e-903acd1a44f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7507d02e-e1da-50a2-9a9d-6236d6a7afae" :question/math-academy-id "q-104745" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,\\pi -1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(\\pi ,\\pi -1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,\\pi -1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot {e}^{\\pi -1}+(\\pi -1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot {e}^{1}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}+0)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5570ad04-a911-54fe-882f-cae6a0eccb35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-{e}^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257514} {:answer/id #uuid "69d52aa2-f5a3-518e-9e37-8487d0ca7564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257719} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdee35c-5f2c-5453-8ea0-524f7e2d5558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257879} {:answer/id #uuid "a641ae1f-fce3-54ae-b9d3-8c7c4dd0c256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}+{e}^{4}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258233} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f247a0-2670-5ca0-8d3b-f9534636b266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bdee35c-5f2c-5453-8ea0-524f7e2d5558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257879} :answer-field/id #uuid "399493dc-69ea-5245-b7ef-1fc5d184a6d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33d927c3-e1a0-5a24-bd3b-65369e4fc02e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124030" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\in [1,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,4].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩, \\\\ r(4) & =⟨4,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(4,4)-f(2,1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}+{e}^{4\\cdot 4})-({2}^{2}+{e}^{2\\cdot 1}) \\\\  & =(16+{e}^{16})-(4+{e}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69c13dff-cd6b-59c8-8afc-6e8f8eabf8f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324856} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0e1662-5531-5797-9a2d-180d1aefa39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{ln}^{2}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325032} {:answer/id #uuid "b060de89-9a07-5c08-8a5a-902923743941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325474} {:answer/id #uuid "df7d018d-9bcc-56b5-9a57-3f93a49339b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325922} {:answer/id #uuid "fba1ed0a-e6ae-5b81-9e9c-40ba51cc6012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fba1ed0a-e6ae-5b81-9e9c-40ba51cc6012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326186} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5eacfc-70b8-5d6e-b1f9-f5af4734a6b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73c892c9-a1e1-5820-84c2-50c996c14b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-39349" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for $x\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{1}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
